    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Marks of a whole class"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "A teacher has marks of 100 students grouped in class intervals. Listing them all is tedious; the mean of grouped data gives a quick average."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "a + (Σfu/Σf) h",
          "a + Σfd/Σf",
          "choose a method",
          "class intervals",
          "class mark",
          "class mark assumption",
          "make a table",
          "mean = Σfx / Σf"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "In grouped data, observations are put into class intervals with frequencies.\nThe class mark is the mid-point: (upper limit + lower limit) divided by 2.\nDirect method: mean = sum of f x divided by sum of f, where x is the class mark.\nAssumed mean method: pick a convenient a and let d = x - a. Then mean = a + Σfd / Σf.\nStep deviation method: with class width h, let u = (x - a)/h. Then mean = a + (Σfu/Σf) x h.\nThese assume that the frequency of each class is centred at its class mark.\nMake a table with columns for class, frequency, class mark and the product, then total the columns.\nUse the method that gives the smallest numbers to avoid arithmetic mistakes."
        }
      },
